    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-78682",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559",
      "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/e5f0d56383e7cdc7bece094bb3a770695bf87f6d2bf6c4dce5876f4432b56803.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$",
      "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/0b38ee461b8895898a73f2b2120e1dac9823b1bf19b46924786c5c27c9beae74.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]",
